y. Long waiting-time outliers in the manipulation literature are consistent with heavy carbon, but the primary work also discusses alternative histories. Slowness alone is not an established isotope identification. arxiv.orgWhat the common physical model supports—and where it failsI constructed a reduced model in which productive exchange and carbon loss arise from the same electron–nucleus collision distribution.The inputs were relativistic energy transfer, a thermal nuclear-velocity distribution, and the published effective exchange and knockout thresholds. Isotope mass changes collision kinematics and nuclear motion; it is not assigned an independent desired hopping rate. The scattering and vibrational framework has direct primary precedent. arxiv.org+1The added assumptions are consequential:A local harmonic surrogate supplies the isotope dependence of nuclear velocities. It does not replace a phonon calculation for the actual mixed-isotope configuration.Effective energy windows classify exchange and loss. They do not resolve all reaction branches within the exchange window.The full construction pilot recognises individual exchange events.A 50:50 independent isotope inventory is assumed for that pilot; it is not inferred from the mere availability of mixed-isotope CVD graphene.Beam tails were included. The published probe analysis has substantial extended intensity, so I did not grant a perfectly isolated target atom. The pilot used an approximate neighbour-to-target intensity ratio of 0.06, guided by that profile, rather than treating the estimate as an exact measurement of the later manipulation experiment. Nature CommunicationsAfter calibration to the reported light-carbon hopping rate at 55 keV, this reduced model predicted an isotope-rate contrast of about 29. That is a model output, not a measured contrast. A cross-check at 60 keV did not reproduce the nominal reported manipulation rate, so the model is not quantitatively validated across beam energies.The complete conditional pilot was expensiveI ran the read–prepare–write–verify sequence, including unintended exchanges, their physical reversal and competing carbon loss.Under the assumptions above, 813 of 2,000 attempted cells completed a correct three-site edit and verification. Successful runs averaged about 98 exchanges and 11 hours of modelled irradiation time, before a complete accounting of real imaging overhead.Those numbers are useful for allocation even though they are not experimental yield predictions:The 24-exchange algebraic operation understates the processing cost.Finding and verifying the isotope arrangement can cost more than writing it.Beam-tail recovery is a material part of the operation.The favourable calculation supports a small scientific manipulation before it supports any scalable process.The model also omits a reaction distinction that can invalidate its isotope bookkeeping. Its numerical success fraction must therefore remain a conditional diagnostic, not a claimed manufacturing yield.The decisive chemical interface: an intact image can conceal the wrong transferThe primary Si–C trajectory study contains both direct exchanges and trajectories in which carbon is left temporarily as a nearby adatom. It also reports four-coordinate silicon sites returning to three-coordinate sites through carbon addition. The associated energetic attraction is evidence for favourable configurations, not a measurement of the probability that the original carbon returns. arxiv.orgFor isotope writing, at least three histories must be distinguished:Microscopic historyConsequence for the intended isotope mappingDirect exchange with the struck carbon retainedImplements the intended swapTemporary detachment followed by return of that same carbon to the required siteCan implement the intended swapReplacement or different rebonding during the excursionMay restore the visible structure while changing the intended mass patternThere is no basis here for treating every adatom excursion as destructive. There is also no basis for treating every restored three-coordinate image as proof of faithful transfer.Independent graphene experiments reinforce the observation problem: vacancy healing by mobile carbon adatoms can become faster than the imaging process, and the observed damage rate then differs from the underlying vacancy-creation rate. Their analysis explicitly couples the finite scanning probe to healing dynamics. arxiv.orgThe manufacturing model consequently needs more information than a silicon transition rate. Schematically,Wx,m→x′,m′=Φ∑jwj∫Qj(Γ) P(x′,m′∣x,m,j,Γ) dΓ.W_{x,m\rightarrow x',m'}
